# Copy to interface/.env.local, which is gitignored. Never commit a real value from here.
#
# Only the alert subscription write path needs a database. Every other number the interface
# renders comes from the chain, and the app runs without one — the alerts form stays disabled.
# The font variable below is different: without it a *fresh clone* cannot build at all.

# --- alerts ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Neon, the POOLED connection string — the host with `-pooler` in it. Route handlers run as
# serverless functions and open a connection per invocation; the direct endpoint runs out of
# connections under load, the pooled one is built for exactly this. The relayer takes the
# direct string instead, because it is one long-lived process. Same database either way.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@ep-xxxx-pooler.REGION.aws.neon.tech/DBNAME?sslmode=require

# The chain the portal writes subscriptions for. Must match the deployment the app is pointed
# at — a subscription carries the registry address, so one made against a superseded set is
# inert rather than wrong. See docs/contract.md §8.
# The interface defaults to mainnet, so leaving this unset points it at BOT Chain (677).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_NETWORK=mainnet

# --- the licensed heading face -----------------------------------------------
# Beautifully Delicious Sans is bought per-licence and is gitignored, because publishing the
# woff2 in a public repo is redistribution and the webfont licence does not cover it. A clone
# therefore has no font, and `next build` fails on the missing import.
#
# Set this on the build machine (Vercel: Project Settings -> Environment Variables) and
# scripts/restore-font.js writes the file back before the build. An env var stays private to
# the project, so the file reaches our build and our origin and nobody else — which is the
# same thing self-hosting does, and what the licence actually permits.
#
# Locally you do not need this: put your licensed copy in interface/app/fonts/ and the script
# leaves it alone. Generate the value from the file itself, never by hand:
#   PowerShell: [Convert]::ToBase64String([IO.File]::ReadAllBytes((Resolve-Path "app\fonts\BeautifullyDeliciousSans-Black.woff2"))) | Set-Clipboard
#     (Resolve-Path is not decoration: [IO.File] resolves a relative path against the process
#      directory, not PowerShell's, so without it this reads from the wrong folder or throws.)
#   bash:       base64 -w0 app/fonts/BeautifullyDeliciousSans-Black.woff2
#
# BEAUTIFULLY_DELICIOUS_B64=wOF2...
